 Top of the Morning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photo: Daniel Maletzki/EUS-Nachrichten/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Nvidia this morning reinstated Jensen Huang as president and CEO, effective immediately.

 The big picture: This is what many in Silicon Valley had wanted, and what many on Wall Street thought was nearly impossible.

 Investor reax: Nvidia shares are up around 30% on the news, representing around $900 billion in added value.

 What they're saying: "I am honored to return to Nvidia and lead one of the most consequential technology companies in the world at the beginning of a new industrial revolution," said Huang in the press release. "I take this responsibility with the greatest humility and renewed commitment."

 Board chair Harvey Jones adds: "The Board believes Jensen is the right person to lead Nvidia, and that his reinstatement is in the best interests of our stockholders, employees, partners and customers." Also: "The Board received input from senior levels of the United States government, and based on that input, the Board is confident that Mr. Huang's reinstatement poses no national security risk." The intrigue: The company's independent investigation into Huang remains ongoing, which could put Nvidia in a box if its ultimate findings are in any way disqualifying for Huang.

 Don't be surprised if Congress wants a peek at what Nvidia finds (and maybe at how hard it looked). In terms of WH palace intrigue, it seems that Howard Lutnick bested Scott Bessent, which perhaps we should have seen coming yesterday when Bessent softened his earlier statements. Zoom out: This is obviously huge news for AI trade momentum, but may just be a short-term sugar high.

 Hyperscaler capex is flattening out , as AI profits remain elusive for almost all but those selling equipment to build and fill data centers (e.g., Nvidia). Huang also is said to have become more conservative on investments in the weeks before his ouster, so his return doesn't necessarily mean a return to the Wild West of circular financing. Particularly as costs of capital increase. Look ahead: Huang is expected to appear before House committees next week for a closed-door transcribed interview, and then at a future date for a public Senate Banking Committee hearing. All of that just got even more interesting.

 
 
